  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "num": "01",
      "en": "{Music} has long been considered to be an enjoyable {pastime} for many people.Attending a {concert} or a music {festival} brings us {wonderful} experiences.It is also highly popular due to the {individualized} effects on relieving stress and anxiety-and its {mental} health benefits can't be argued. {Musicians} create healing sounds with their {instruments} .For example, the {quiet} sounds of a {piano} or {violin} create a {peaceful} atmosphere that calms the mind.",
      "cn": "长期以来， <u> 音乐 </u> 一直被许多人视为一种令人愉悦的 <u> 消遣 </u> 方式。参加 <u> 音乐会 </u> 或音乐 <u> 节 </u> 会给我们带来 <u> 美妙的 </u> 体验。它也因其在缓解压力和焦虑方面具有 <u> 个性化 </u> 的效果而备受欢迎——而且音乐在 <u> 心理 </u> 健康方面的益处是毋庸置疑的。 <u> 音乐家 </u> 用他们的 <u> 乐器 </u> 创造出有治愈效果的声音。比如， <u> 钢琴 </u> 或 <u> 小提琴 </u> 的 <u> 轻柔 </u> 音色会营造出一种抚慰心灵的 <u> 宁静 </u> 氛围。"
    },
    {
      "num": "02",
      "en": "Some {experts} say that music can be harmful if it is too {noisy} .However, at moderate volumes, music can be very beneficial if one is in pain.When we feel {anxious} , listening to our favorite tunes can bring a sense of {harmony} to our minds.This, of course, can lead to less pain.Even after {surgery} , patients often recover faster when they listen to soothing melodies ( (旋律) ).",
      "cn": "一些 <u> 专家 </u> 表示，如果音乐过于 <u> 嘈杂 </u> ，可能会对人有害。然而，在适中的音量下，如果一个人处于疼痛之中，音乐也可能非常有益。当我们感到 <u> 焦虑 </u> 时，聆听自己喜欢的旋律可以为我们的心灵带来 <u> 和谐 </u> 的感受。这自然能够减轻疼痛。甚至在 <u> 手术 </u> 后，患者在聆听舒缓的旋律时，也能更快地康复。"
    },
    {
      "num": "03",
      "en": "Many people enjoy relaxing music in the {evening} {prior} to going to bed.They say that listening to {gentle} music before bedtime can play a big role in calming the brain enough to sleep, helping them fall {asleep} more easily.While the {validity} of the idea is still being assessed, the lowered stress can even be {tied} back to blood pressure.Similarly, according to researchers, listening to just {thirty} minutes of soft music every day may help with healthy blood {sugar} levels, through the lowering of stress and anxiety.",
      "cn": "许多人喜欢在 <u> 晚上 </u> 睡觉 <u> 前 </u> 听舒缓的音乐。他们说，睡前聆听 <u> 柔和的 </u> 音乐能够对大脑起到很大的安抚作用，从而更容易 <u> 入睡 </u> 。尽管这一观点的 <u> 可信性 </u> 仍在评估之中，但压力的减轻甚至可能 <u> 与 </u> 血压的变化 <u> 联系 </u> 起来。同样，根据研究人员的说法，每天仅听 <u> 三十 </u> 分钟的轻音乐就可以帮助维持健康的血 <u> 糖 </u> 水平，这是通过减轻压力和焦虑来实现的。"
    },
    {
      "num": "04",
      "en": "When it comes to heart health, there is speculation ( (推测) ) that it's not the style of music, but rather the {tempo} that makes it so good for your heart health.In one {European} study, researchers used music at different speeds to {stimulate} the participants and {monitored} their heart rates and blood pressure.Interestingly, the more {cheerful} the music was, the faster their heart rates were.On the other hand, when the music slowed, the participants {adjusted} to a calmer state, and the effects on heart rates appeared to follow {suit} .",
      "cn": "当谈到心脏健康时，有一种推测认为，对心脏健康有益的并非音乐的风格，而是音乐的 <u> 节奏 </u> 。在一项 <u> 欧洲的 </u> 研究中，研究人员使用不同速度的音乐来 <u> 刺激 </u> 参与者，并 <u> 监测 </u> 他们的心率和血压。有趣的是，音乐越 <u> 欢快 </u> ，他们的心率就越快。另一方面，当音乐放慢时，参与者会 <u> 调节 </u> 至一种更加平和的状态，对心率的影响似乎也 <u> 随之 </u> 改变。"
    },
    {
      "num": "05",
      "en": "The implications of music on overall well-being are {incredible} .It can touch our {soul} and lift our {spirit} .But there is a whole range of other health issues for which turning up the {radio} could be beneficial, which is what makes music so valuable.",
      "cn": "音乐对整体健康的影响是 <u> 令人难以置信的 </u> 。它能触动我们的 <u> 灵魂 </u> ，振奋我们的 <u> 精神 </u> 。但调高 <u> 收音机 </u> 音量（听音乐）可能对一系列其他健康问题也有裨益，这正是音乐如此宝贵的原因。 5NoOncib/NjiX02BpMIzP4m+378Hw1xTRxUdPaP/PFjKUFq4K2G7tp6ZLYoCE601yW1W39VMdLmovFdSHs4nVQ=="
    }
  ],
